Apple's 13-inch MacBook Pro has now been  refreshed for 2020 but it seems like all  the attention is on the higher end  eighteen hundred dollar model since  that's the model that features Intel's  latest tenth gen processors but I think  people are way over exaggerating how  important those chips really are I've  even seen people recommending the 2020  MacBook Air over the base MacBook Pro  just because of the tension processors  which is a massive mistake and I'll  explain why in just a minute now I'm not  saying that the new $1,800 MacBook Pro  isn't a great choice in fact I think  it's definitely worth the extra $500  over the 20/20 base model mainly because  you get a 512 guess SD and 16 gigs of  ram included which those upgrades used  to cost $400 alone and on top of that  you get other great features like much  better speakers better cooling thanks to  an extra fan to extra ports and of  course better performance but the  reality is that not everyone wants to  spend almost $2,000 for a macbook and  for those who can afford the $1,300 pro  they'll be making an excellent choice  despite the hñ processor and what  reviewers are saying about it I  personally think the real mistake is  thinking that the 20/20 MacBook Air with  a quad core i5 or even the i7 processor  will have better performance than this  base macbook pro thanks to the  all-powerful 10th gen ship but that's  simply not true  the main reason for that is because  Apple decided not to connect the MacBook  Airs fan to the CPUs heatsink so even  the dual core i3 model hits a hundred  degrees Celsius relatively quickly let  alone the i7 and when we ran our CPU  stress test the MacBook Air suffered  because of the lack of cooling with the  base macbook pro pulley way ahead so  here's my take on the MacBook Air versus  the MacBook Pro if you care about  performance whatsoever by the base  MacBook Pro instead so that means if  you're considering the quad-core  model of the MacBook Air you actually do  care about performance so by the base  MacBook Pro instead trust me it will  perform much better  despite the a gen chip and the fans will  stay quieter as well and yes the  long-awaited fortune' inch MacBook Pro  is in the works but more and more rumors  and leaks are appointing to at least a  spring 2021 release date and the reality  is that a lot of people aren't going to  wait that long so let's get into why I  think this 20/20 base 13-inch MacBook  Pro is still a great choice right now  first off the main issue with the 2019  model was the butterfly keyboard and  that is now fixed with the 2020 model  since it gets the new magic keyboard and  there truly is a massive difference in  the quality of the keyboard and  everything from key travel and typing  experience to reliability as well  and another nice addition is that we now  get a physical Escape key so it's much  more convenient to use than before but  other than with the keyboard you won't  find any other physical difference  except for the tiny increase in weight  and thickness compared to the 2019 model  well of course it'd be nice to have that  14 inch redesigned right now but we  can't blame Apple because the 14 inch  model was never anything more than  rumours from the supply chain and then  blogging sites took those rumours and  decided to get everybody hyped up making  them think that it's coming this year so  we shouldn't blame Apple we should  actually be thanking them because this  base 8 gen model has become a much  better deal especially for those who  want 16 gigs of ram with a 2019 model if  you wanted 256 gigs of storage and 16  gigabytes of RAM you'd have to spend  $1,700 to get it but now the 256 gig SSD  is already included in the base price  and upgrading to 16 gigs of ram is now  only a $100 upgrade so you're saving  $300 compared to last year to get the  same specs and you're getting the  reliable magic keyboard which is even  more important for some people so we  definitely are getting a pretty good  value this you  with the base MacBook Pro but there's  one question left that we need to answer  does the old eighth generation chip make  this MacBook Pro a bad choice well if  you've seen our recent $1300 versus  $1,800 20/20 MacBook Pro comparison  video you'll know that the tangential  isn't massively faster than the base  eight gen like everyone is making a  sound like we only saw around a 12%  higher Geekbench five CPU score on the  Tenjin which translated to around 24%  better performance in the Cinebench r20  stress test now that is a decent  improvement but if you don't have 1,800  dollars to spend on a MacBook Pro you're  still getting very impressive processing  performance with the base model that'll  actually be enough for a wide variety of  people like programmers photo editors  logic pro users and maybe even video  editors now the big difference between  these two is graphics power where we saw  a 48 percent higher metal graphics score  on the $1,800 model this is actually a  really big difference but keep in mind  that this will only improve graphics  work like video editing and 3d rendering  so photo editors programmers and logic  pro users won't see any significant  performance gains from the extra  graphics power and even for video  editing running the Bruce X test the  $1,800 model was surprisingly only 20%  faster than the base model a much  smaller difference than we expected so  the base model keeps up surprisingly  well but one thing that the better  graphics and the higher-end model does  improve is gaming performance Greg from  Greg's gadgets showed off how that  $1,800 model was able to play some games  at decent frame rates like League of  Legends counter-strike go fortnight and  more but keep in mind that he ran each  of these games at 1440 by 900 resolution  which is only 31 percent of the MacBook  Pros native resolution of 2560 by 1600  so if you want decent FPS for gaming  while at higher resolutions then you're  either gonna have to go for a 16 inch  MacBook Pro which has a dedicated  graphics card or you'll have to set up  an a GPU instead and if you do go the  a GPU route it actually makes much more  sense to buy the base $1,300 model with  16 gigabytes of RAM for $1400  since the e GPU takes the integrated  graphics of the Intel chips out of the  equation completely so if you're  interested to see how that will perform  then click the subscribe button and  enable notifications because that video  is coming soon the point I'm trying to  make is that the difference in  performance between this base model and  the higher-end $1800 model isn't as big  as people are making it out to be the  base model has more than enough  performance for those who can't afford  to spend $1,800 on the higher-end  MacBook Pro however for some people  spending the extra cash really does make  a lot of sense like for video editors or  people who want more value thanks to the  other improvements like better speakers  faster RAM and two more ports but if you  can't see yourself spending that much  cash don't feel bad about choosing the  base model instead I would just strongly  recommend upgrading to 16 gigabytes of  RAM for only $100 it's an excellent deal  and it'll help for everything from video  editing programming photo editing Logic  Pro 10 and also for people like me who  love having a lot of chrome tabs open at  once so with the base macbook pro you're  getting a bright display with great p3  color gamut you'll get the new reliable  magic keyboard an excellent trackpad  more stores than before and much better  real-world performance than any of the  MacBook Airs  even the i7 model now if you're someone  who already has a laptop that's working  just fine and you can easily wait a full  year for the 14-inch redesigned then I  would recommend doing so but for  everyone else who can't wait that long  the base macbook pro is an excellent  laptop for the price if you enjoy this  review go ahead and tap 